---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditRecolorPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditRecolorPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="grayscale-pdf">
  <PdfEditRecolorPdf client:load mode="grayscale" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Why a mono-looking document still bills as colour</h2>
      <p>
        University print shops, libraries and managed office printers charge per page by ink model. A
        page counts as colour if the print job has any non-grey colour on it: a faint blue heading, a
        coloured hyperlink, a logo in the footer, a highlighter mark, or a table rule in dark navy.
      </p>
      <p>
        So a hundred-page dissertation with a coloured header on each page prints as a hundred colour
        pages, at five to ten times the mono rate. Convert the PDF to grayscale first, and the driver
        sends only grey, so the job prices as mono.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The four methods, and when each is right</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[680px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Method</th>
              <th scope="col">What it does</th>
              <th scope="col">Best for</th>
              <th scope="col">Effect on size</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Perceptual grey</td>
              <td>0.2126·R + 0.7152·G + 0.0722·B (the Rec. 709 luma weights)</td>
              <td>Anything with photographs, charts or design</td>
              <td>Usually smaller than colour</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Flat average</td>
              <td>(R + G + B) ÷ 3</td>
              <td>Keeping separation between colours of similar brightness</td>
              <td>Similar</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Pure black and white</td>
              <td>Every pixel above the cut-off becomes white, the rest black</td>
              <td>Text scans, faxes, forms</td>
              <td>Often 70–90% smaller</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Dithered black and white</td>
              <td>Floyd–Steinberg error diffusion into neighbouring pixels</td>
              <td>Photographs that must survive 1-bit output</td>
              <td>Larger than plain 1-bit, far smaller than grey</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Human vision is about three times more sensitive to green than to red, and about ten times more
        sensitive to green than to blue. The perceptual grey weights follow this. A flat average makes
        reds too light and blues too dark, so a photograph converted by averaging looks slightly wrong.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Setting the black-and-white cut-off</h2>
      <p>
        In 1-bit mode, the tool compares each pixel with one brightness value from 0 (black) to 255
        (white). The default of 160 suits a typical scan of white paper with black ink.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>If faint pencil or a light scan disappears, raise the cut-off toward 200 so more of the greyscale stays black.</li>
        <li>If grey or yellowed paper comes out black, lower it toward 110 so the background goes white.</li>
        <li>If a phone photo has shadows down one edge, no single cut-off fixes it. Use perceptual grey, or scan again flat.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The preview uses the same pixel pipeline as the export, at screen resolution, so it matches the
        output. Hold the compare button to see the original.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The cost: your text stops being text</h2>
      <p>
        A browser cannot recolour a PDF's content without redrawing its pages. The output is a picture
        of each page, so it has no selectable text, search, copy and paste, screen-reader access or
        working links. Keep the colour original for search, and use the grayscale version for print.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you only need a smaller file, use <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a>. It can often
        reduce a document a lot and keeps the text layer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Will the file get bigger or smaller?</h2>
      <p>
        Both can happen. It depends on the original.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A colour scan converted to 1-bit black and white usually gets much smaller. Flat two-tone
          images compress very well.
        </li>
        <li>
          A brochure with many photos converted to grey gets a little smaller, because two of the three
          colour channels are gone.
        </li>
        <li>
          A compact text-only PDF converted to grey almost always gets bigger. A page of text stored as
          font instructions is a few kilobytes. The same page as a 150 DPI image is a few hundred.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>The result panel shows the size before and after.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Related</h2>
      <p>
        For a dark background for night reading, see
        <a href="/invert-pdf-colors">Invert PDF Colors</a>, which uses the same rendering pipeline. To
        desaturate one image, <a href="/grayscale-image">Grayscale Image</a> is faster. To turn PDF
        pages into image files, try <a href="/pdf-to-jpg">PDF to JPG</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
